

MONETIZATION Cyber Fleet Services - RoboReady 
RoboReady is essentially the “is this property suitable?” layer, while Cyber Fleet 
Services is the “turn this property into an operating Cybercab/charging asset” layer. 
RoboReady currently sells one-time property assessments at $499, $2,500, and $7,500, 
while Cyber Fleet Services is looking for vehicle owners, commercial properties, and 
existing charger owners. RRoboReady+1 
The strongest monetization model 
I would position the partnership as: 
RoboReady finds and qualifies the sites. Cyber Fleet Services converts the qualified 
sites into revenue-producing fleet infrastructure. 
1. Referral / lead-generation revenue 
This is probably the easiest model to launch. 
RoboReady adds a “Monetize Your Property” CTA after an assessment: 
Your property may be suitable for a Cybercab charging & staging hub. 
Connect with Cyber Fleet Services to evaluate leasing, charging, and fleet deployment. 
Cyber Fleet Services pays RoboReady for qualified leads. 
For example: 
● $100–$250 per qualified property lead 
● $500–$2,000 for a site that reaches a formal feasibility stage 
● $2,500–$10,000+ for a site that actually signs a lease/development agreement 
The exact economics would obviously need to be negotiated. 
This works particularly well because RoboReady already collects the information Cyber 
Fleet Services needs during its assessment: property characteristics, site configuration, 
charging requirements, and infrastructure needs. RoboReady explicitly provides 
infrastructure planning and site concepts in its higher tiers. RRoboReady 
 
2. Revenue share on every deployed site 

This is the model I'd pursue if you want recurring revenue rather than just lead fees. 
Suppose RoboReady identifies a property and Cyber Fleet Services subsequently turns 
it into a Cybercab/charging hub. 
RoboReady could receive: 
5–10% of Cyber Fleet Services' net revenue from the site for 3–5 years 
or potentially: 
1–3% of gross site revenue for the life of the agreement. 
That makes RoboReady an actual origination channel for Cyber Fleet Services. 
The pitch becomes: 
“We'll identify, score, and qualify the real estate. You operate and monetize it. We share 
the economics.” 
That is considerably more compelling than simply selling them leads. 
 
3. Bundle RoboReady directly into Cyber Fleet Services' 
property onboarding 
Cyber Fleet Services currently says its onboarding process is: 
Apply → Onboard → Go live → Get paid, with the company assessing the asset before 
deployment. CCyber Fleet Services 
RoboReady could become the front-end assessment engine for that process. 
For example: 
Cyber Fleet Site Qualification 
$499 RoboReady assessment 
Then: 
Cyber Fleet Services feasibility review 
Then: 

Site agreement / deployment 
Instead of Cyber Fleet Services having to build its own property assessment 
infrastructure, it effectively white-labels RoboReady. 
Potential commercial arrangement: 
● Cyber Fleet Services buys assessments wholesale. 
● RoboReady charges $250–$400/site. 
● Cyber Fleet Services bundles them into its acquisition process. 
● Or Cyber Fleet Services pays RoboReady only when a site progresses. 
This could become a much larger relationship than referrals. 
 
