6. The really interesting opportunity: build a 
marketplace 
Longer term, I wouldn't make RoboReady merely an assessment company. 
I'd make it the marketplace connecting autonomous mobility demand with physical 
infrastructure. 
Something like: 
Property → RoboReady Score → Infrastructure requirements → Fleet operator → 
Deployment → Revenue 
Cyber Fleet Services becomes one of the operators on the platform. 
Potentially there could eventually be: 
● Cyber Fleet Services 
● other autonomous fleet operators 
● charging operators 
● parking operators 
● property developers 
● hotels 
● shopping centers 
● airports 
● hospitals 
● multifamily owners 
RoboReady becomes the qualification/data layer connecting them. 
That's potentially much more valuable than selling $499 assessments. 
 
7. A particularly attractive commercial 
structure 
If I were structuring this partnership, I'd propose three layers: 

Revenue stream RoboReady gets Cyber Fleet gets 
Qualified lead $250–$1,000 Qualified property 
Completed assessment $499–$7,500 Standardized site intelligence 
Successful deployment 1–5% revenue share New fleet infrastructure 
Portfolio customer Recurring SaaS fee Pipeline of qualified sites 
The sweet spot is combining the first three. 
For example: 
$500 qualified-lead fee + $2,500 assessment + 2% deployment revenue share 
That gives RoboReady revenue immediately while also creating substantial upside 
when a site becomes economically valuable. 
 
